WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.722
Axis is one of the most important NumPy concepts, and it is also one of the easiest to

00:00:06.722 --> 00:00:08.857
confuse at first.

00:00:08.857 --> 00:00:14.867
For a two-dimensional array, axis zero performs a calculation down the rows and returns one result for each

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:21.292
column. Axis one performs a calculation across the columns and returns one result for each row.

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:25.527
In this example, click Run and compare the two outputs.

00:00:25.527 --> 00:00:28.137
The column means use axis zero.

00:00:28.137 --> 00:00:32.317
We have three columns, so the result contains three averages.

00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:35.092
The row means use axis one.

00:00:35.092 --> 00:00:39.322
We have four rows, so the result contains four averages.

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:44.217
A useful practical question is: what do I want one result for?

00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:49.027
If you want one result for each column, you will often use axis zero.

00:00:49.027 --> 00:00:54.017
If you want one result for each row, you will often use axis one.

00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:59.037
When you are unsure, look at the shape before and after the operation and connect each dimension to

00:00:59.037 --> 00:01:01.420
its meaning.
